b-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-025", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Strabo and Plutarch called Artashat the Armenian Carthage. Founded in 176 BC on a peninsula of nine hills where two rivers meet, it still shows the remains of its great walls."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-025 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Strabo and Plutarch called the city the Armenian Carthage [S5]; it was founded in 176 BC [S1, S3]; it stood on a peninsula of nine hills [S3]; the peninsula lies where two rivers meet (confluence of the Araks and Metsamor) [S3]; remains of its great walls are still visible [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-073 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Artashat (Artaxata) is an ancient city, a major city and capital of the ancient Kingdom of Armenia (central claim: an ancient city) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artaxata]; Its ruins are in Armenia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artaxata]; Strabo and Plutarch called it the Armenian Carthage [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artaxata]; It was founded in 176 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artaxata]; It was founded on a peninsula of nine hills [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artaxata]; The peninsula lies where two rivers (Araks and Metsamor) meet [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artaxata]; The remains of the great walls of the city are still visible today [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artaxata]"}]'::jsonb),
